From 20158014477c730e632e4ff472d216361a0da2a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Szpakowski <michi.szpakowski@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 15:51:35 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH 1/2] ALSA: hda/cs8409: Forget the cached I2C device
 address on suspend

cs8409_set_i2c_dev_addr() writes the I2C address register of the CS8409
only when the address differs from the one cached in spec->dev_addr, and
nothing ever invalidates that cache.

If the CS8409 loses its register contents while the codec is suspended,
the address register is back at its reset value and the cache still
holds the address of the device accessed last. With a single companion
codec on the bus every access after resume then skips the write and
goes out with the wrong address.

Seen on an Apple MacBookPro13,1, a CS8409 with a CS42L83 and four
SSM3515 amplifiers, where the codec loses power in S3. The CS42L83 was
the device accessed last before the suspend. At the init after resume
its device ID read as zeroes; the amplifiers, addressed after it,
answered, and so did the CS42L83 from then on, the address register
having been written for the amplifiers in between.

Invalidate the cache in the suspend path.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
---
 sound/hda/codecs/cirrus/cs8409.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/hda/codecs/cirrus/cs8409.c b/sound/hda/codecs/cirrus/cs8409.c
index c43ff3ef7..ce02fcd9f 100644
--- a/sound/hda/codecs/cirrus/cs8409.c
+++ b/sound/hda/codecs/cirrus/cs8409.c
@@ -1027,6 +1027,13 @@ static int cs8409_cs42l42_suspend(struct hda_codec *codec)
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&spec->i2c_clk_work);
 	cs8409_disable_i2c_clock(codec);
 
+	/*
+	 * The CS8409 may lose power while suspended, and its I2C address
+	 * register with it. With one device on the bus the cached address
+	 * would never be written again.
+	 */
+	spec->dev_addr = 0;
+
 	snd_hda_shutup_pins(codec);
 
 	return 0;
-- 
